"""Consolidated study logic; isolated sections preserve each check's local state."""
from types import SimpleNamespace as _Namespace
_sections = {}


def _make_build_revision_text():
    """Generate substantive journal extensions from live revision result tables."""
    from pathlib import Path
    import json, csv

    def write_extension(Q):
        D = Q / 'docs'
        R = Q / 'results/v2'
        p = json.loads((R / 'program_summary.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
        v = json.loads((R / 'version_summary.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
        phase = list(csv.DictReader((R / 'ablation_summary.csv').open(encoding='utf-8')))
        rows = []
        for n in ['1000', '10000', '100000']:
            values = {r['phase']: r for r in phase if r['n'] == n and r['witness'] == 'True'}
            rows.append('| ' + n + ' | ' + ' | '.join((f"{float(values[k]['median_ms']):.3f}" for k in ['construction_ms', 'sweep_excluding_witness_ms', 'witness_ms', 'assembly_and_hash_ms', 'serialization_ms', 'total_ms'])) + ' |')
        ablation = '\n'.join(['| N | Construction | Sweep excluding witness | Witness | Assembly and hash | Serialization | Total |', '|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|'] + rows)
        source_rows = list(csv.DictReader((R / 'program_results.csv').open(encoding='utf-8')))
        programtable = ['| Source program | Runs | Nonconforming | Snapshot | Grid | Guard-complete |', '|---|---|---|---|---|---|']
        for name in sorted({r['program'] for r in source_rows}):
            rr = [r for r in source_rows if r['program'] == name]
            bad = [r for r in rr if r['conformance_failure'] == 'True']
            programtable.append(f"| {name} | {len(rr)} | {len(bad)} | {sum((r['snapshot_detected'] == 'True' for r in bad))} | {sum((r['grid_detected'] == 'True' for r in bad))} | {len(bad)} |")
        text = "## Appendix A Program conformance and the semantic information boundary\n\nThe first evaluation verifies declared old/new selector semantics. That question is useful when both implementations have faithful finite contracts, but it does not establish that an actual executable conforms to its advertised contract. We therefore add a separate program-output study. The expected specification remains unchanged for every program: whole-score-group threshold candidates, inclusive empirical precision, and maximum TP followed by maximum selected count. We never pass a mutant specification to the checker in this study.\n\nThe system under test is an executable Python module separate from the production policy comparator and independent oracle. Its build routine constructs actual Boolean candidate masks and integer counts. Its selection routine calls its own feasibility guard and uses its own priority code. Six source variants change those executable operations: strict precision, strict score thresholding, reversed secondary priority, omission of a middle group, inside-tie prefixes, and an incremented feasibility TP count. The generated source files and their SHA-256 values are retained. Tests execute the reference and each variant on the same 30 fitted-pipeline inputs. These are source mutants of a local selector, not six pre-existing products or a naturally occurring defect collection.\n\nThe adapter instruments calls to the executable feasibility guard and records the actual k/n values it receives. It obtains these values from the running program rather than from a supplied `new_spec`. It combines those events with the unchanged reference oracle's critical ratios and the policy-domain endpoints. It then calls the executable at every resulting critical point and open-cell representative. Expected memberships come from direct threshold-mask enumeration under the reference specification. Snapshot and thirteen-point baselines call that very same executable, receive the same observed output IDs, and use the same expected specification. Their distinction is policy coverage, so this comparison removes the semantic-information asymmetry of comparing score-only tools with a configured mutant model.\n\nThis adapter has an explicit and substantial trust boundary. The corpus is audited to use alpha only as an argument to the instrumented guard; an AST check enforces that syntactic restriction for the selection routine. Candidate construction and priority do not depend on alpha. The adapter checks that guard events remain the same at all evaluated policies. Under these conditions, program outcomes cannot change between the collected guard values. The argument would fail for hidden alpha-dependent branches, stateful selection, numerical precision guards not represented by the trace, or an uninstrumented external function. A consistent trace at sampled points alone would not prove event completeness for arbitrary code. We claim coverage for this audited corpus, not a universal dynamic-analysis theorem.\n\nInstruments do not replace the executable's Boolean result: the wrapper records guard inputs and delegates the comparison to the program's original guard. Thus the strict-precision source variant keeps its strict comparison. Candidate-family and objective mutants also run their own changed code. The unchanged oracle specification supplies the desired behavior; it does not supply the changed implementation's semantics. Each certificate records the executable source hash, frozen input hash, collected guard values, complete probe list, expected and actual membership bitsets, and an observation-ID witness when they disagree. A replay audit re-executes every stored observation and verifies the bitsets and source binding.\n\nPROGRAM_TABLE\n\nAcross 210 program/input executions, 136 fail reference conformance. The guard-complete adapter detects those 136, the thirteen-point baseline detects 111, and the alpha=0.50 snapshot detects 72. All 30 reference controls conform. The remaining source-mutant runs are equivalent only on their evaluated fixed input and domain; this is not global program equivalence. These totals are separate from, and must not be pooled with, the earlier 183/183 declared-semantics results. This study establishes that the method can consume outputs of real executable variants without being handed the mutant specification. It remains a local source-mutation experiment, and does not answer how well it integrates with an unrelated industrial selector.\n\n## Appendix B Fresh dependency version executions\n\nWe execute actual SciPy 1.15.3 and 1.18.1 binaries in separate pre-existing Python environments. The worker asserts its loaded SciPy version, records Python and NumPy versions, executable and module paths, extracts and hashes the interpolation implementation, and hashes its generated score arrays. It reads frozen interpolation knots, calibration values, raw tuning scores and labels; those inputs are packaged with individual hashes. The ten cold-chain calibrators and the public WDBC pilot are included without selecting cases on the new result. Training and calibrator fitting are not repeated or changed during this comparison.\n\nBoth workers call `scipy.interpolate.interp1d` on the same clipped queries and calibration arrays. Cold-chain outputs are cast to float32 as in the inherited protocol; the public pilot retains its original output dtype. We compare older 1.15.3 outputs to newer 1.18.1 outputs under the same default selector contract on [0.30,0.90]. This direction is explicitly named because the earlier artifact sometimes used the newer environment as its reference. Policy divergence is symmetric, but old/new witness membership and some threshold descriptions are directional.\n\nAll eleven worker comparisons exhibit at least one changed score, and nine exhibit some policy divergence over the agreed domain. The exact certificates are independently checked against their actual version-produced arrays. This is fresh execution evidence for sensitivity to real dependency implementations on inherited fixed data. It is stronger provenance than an engineered float32 cast, but it is not eleven independent deployments, eleven new datasets, or nine confirmed library bugs. Different numerical implementations may both satisfy their advertised numerical requirements. In the absence of an upstream issue, specified bug-fix behavior or application requirement forbidding these changes, we report a version differential and a potential application regression, not a defect in SciPy.\n\nDefault standalone reproduction re-evaluates the frozen version-worker outputs and their certificates. Re-executing both binaries requires separately installed versions, because a single Python environment cannot load the two distributions reliably as the same module name. The worker commands and environment requirements are explicit. This optional binary-reexecution requirement does not affect regeneration of the main 98-case and mutation studies from the standalone ZIP. We executed both binaries locally in this revision and preserved their fresh logs; a future reproducer is not required to trust that history to re-evaluate the included score arrays.\n\n## Appendix C Algorithmic cost decomposition\n\nEnd-to-end timings do not identify which part of a certificate computation consumes time. We add a within-invocation ablation with instrumented stage boundaries in the production core. Construction includes input validation, sorting, candidate profiling, exact breakpoint construction and rank summaries. The sweep phase excludes time measured inside witness scans. Witness time records only scans for distinct divergent winning-state pairs. Assembly includes PSC computation and input hashing. Serialization measures JSON encoding outside the core. The certificate returned by an instrumented call is asserted equal to the uninstrumented result on a dedicated test; profiling fields are not inserted into the certificate or used by its logic.\n\nThe ablation uses three N/G settings, witness disabled/enabled, and ten rounds per condition, for 60 raw observations. Inputs are prepared outside the timer. Each condition receives a warmup and witness-mode order is randomized within a round. Unlike the original end-to-end traced protocol, this experiment disables tracemalloc. Stage medians below therefore belong to a distinct protocol and should not replace or be pooled with the earlier traced measurements.\n\nABLATION_TABLE\n\nValues are milliseconds for witness-enabled divergent workloads. Each record also stores the measured end-to-end total and an unattributed residual. The validation checks that phase sums plus that residual equal the same invocation's total, and that witness-disabled calls record zero witness time. Medians of component stages generally need not sum to the median of totals; we do not force that identity or infer witness overhead by subtracting independent medians. The extra clock reads themselves incur overhead, and scheduler variation affects short phases.\n\nThe decomposition exposes both sorting/breakpoint work and output/witness work. Witness scans scale with the number of distinct divergent states, not just observation count. A low-cost invariant scan cannot stand in for successful witness construction. Likewise, an efficient policy sweep does not imply a constant-cost independently checkable certificate: hashing, serialization and an external mask-enumeration checker still consume resources. We keep algorithmic asymptotics, measured Python implementation costs, and the slower independent checker as separate claims.\n\n## Appendix D Relation to existing verification and testing methods\n\nParameter-region verification predates this work. Storm's official parametric-model documentation demonstrates region checking and parameter-space partitioning [6]. Junges and colleagues study parameter synthesis and coverage of parameter space for Markov models [7]. Their models, probability properties and region procedures differ from deterministic threshold membership, but they rule out presenting region partitioning itself as our invention. We do not benchmark Storm as though it were a drop-in selected-membership comparator; such a comparison would require a faithful encoding and a common property.\n\nRegression verification also already studies equivalence of closely related program versions [8]. Our restricted finite-input selector contract makes its policy critical values explicit and directly enumerable; it does not solve general program equivalence. The program-output experiment covers a known guard-constrained corpus rather than establishing a general-purpose regression verifier. Treating the old version as a de facto specification may preserve an old bug; applications that require correctness rather than compatibility need an agreed reference contract.\n\nThe separation between a producer and a checker is likewise established. Alkassar and colleagues describe verification of certifying computations [9]. Necula and Schneck's proof-carrying-code work studies machine-checkable safety evidence and the trusted checker boundary [10]. Our certificates are concrete finite membership evidence checked by another implementation. They are not machine-checked proofs of arbitrary executable safety, and their Python checker is not a formally verified trusted kernel. An input digest prevents silent accidental mismatches but neither authenticates the original observations nor proves adapter faithfulness.\n\nTesting ML implementations through metamorphic properties and mutation analysis is also established. The 2021 work on stability-derived relations targets linear-classification programs [11], while Xie and colleagues study classifier implementation testing and actual faults in Weka [12]. Those programs and properties differ from our fixed-label threshold selector. Their empirical designs motivate evaluating executed code and avoiding an inference from model accuracy to program correctness. Our expanded corpus addresses direct execution for a local selector, but remains weaker external-validity evidence than a curated set of independently documented, naturally occurring defects.\n\nThe comparison below records what is demonstrated or discussed in the checked sources. “Not established here” means no matching capability was established in our inspected material; it is not proof that a tool or research area cannot support it. Complexity statements are for different mathematical objects and are not directly comparable.\n\n| Approach | Domain and boundary treatment | Changed selector code | Checkable evidence | Complexity and comparison scope |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Snapshot / fixed grid | Only sampled policies; no full-domain guarantee | Observes executable outputs at probes | Output differences | Number of probes times SUT cost |\n| Score B4 | Full score-induced candidate family, no alpha-region verdict | Does not inspect selector code | Structural equality Boolean | Structural task rather than policy conformance |\n| Storm / parameter synthesis [6,7] | Parametric probabilistic regions; exact setup and coverage depend on procedure | Requires a model of program behavior | Model/property analysis | Not the same selector input problem; no latency ranking claimed |\n| Regression verification [8] | Equivalence of related programs, beyond a fixed alpha grid | Central research target | Verification results under its method | General program equivalence is not reduced to our N/G bound |\n| Certifying computations / PCC [9,10] | Witness/checker and proof trust design, not this alpha contract | Depends on supplied proofs or verified computation | Core purpose | General framework, not a competing selector benchmark |\n| ML metamorphic testing [11,12] | Relations across executions, not this full-domain contract | Executed classifier implementations | Violated relations and mutation evidence | A complementary testing methodology |\n| Our declared-semantics certificate | Exact rational endpoints and open cells for fixed candidate contracts | Requires a faithful finite adapter | Input-bound interval and ID witness | O(N log N+G log G+K+ND), unit-cost fraction assumption |\n| Our executed-program adapter | Complete for alpha-only-in-guard source corpus | Calls changed source directly; no new_spec | Executable hash and replayable membership probes | Depends on traced guards and executed SUT cost; no arbitrary-code guarantee |\n\nA focused search across these five areas expands the primary-source basis and clarifies the closest conceptual overlap. It is still not a systematic review with exhaustive database coverage and forward/backward citation closure. The appropriate novelty claim is the specialized integration of endpoint-correct finite policy equivalence, verifiable membership evidence and explicitly scoped testing adapters. A first-in-literature claim remains unsupported. The expanded paper and artifact are provided for further technical review rather than labeled ready for acceptance at a particular journal.\n\n## Appendix E Reproduction and assurance levels\n\nThe original standalone failure arose because the experiment driver read a historical 255-row grid CSV from a neighboring submission tree. The corrected driver reads a bundled `inputs/historical/` copy and asserts its checksum from the input manifest before any comparison. Default `run_all.py` works from frozen arrays within the extracted artifact; it does not refit models, reinstall dependency versions or need the old submission directory. Optional model refitting is a separate command with its source-data requirements documented. The distinction prevents the term “standalone” from silently including unbundled training assets.\n\nAssurance has several layers. Unit/exhaustive tests validate finite implementations on small and randomized cases. The independent certificate checker reconstructs actual threshold masks on all stored application and declared-mutant certificates. The program adapter calls a separate executable and checks the unchanged reference contract under an explicit guard-coverage restriction. Fresh version workers establish what two actual dependency binaries computed. Checksummed packaging and a clean extraction run test delivery rather than mathematical correctness. None of these layers, alone or together, establishes authenticity of upstream measurements, population model quality, a universally correct verifier or an industrial real-bug detection rate.\n\nThe previous artifact is retained as a historical ZIP. Revised raw records are regenerated rather than mixed with old timings. Source snapshots bind the producer used for those records. A final manifest is generated after documents, evidence and reports have been assembled. The clean-run report names the extracted path, absence of neighboring SUBMISSION_PACKAGE, interpreter, stage exit codes and resulting logical summaries. It is a local clean reproduction, not independent human replication. A separate upstream-confirmed bug corpus and deployment study remain work outside the evidence completed here.\n".replace('PROGRAM_TABLE', '\n'.join(programtable)).replace('ABLATION_TABLE', ablation)
        (D / 'JOURNAL_EXTENSION_V2.md').write_text(text, encoding='utf-8')
        refs = '\n[6] Storm project. Running Storm on Parametric Models. Official documentation. https://www.stormchecker.org/documentation/usage/running-storm-on-parametric-models.html\n\n[7] Junges, S.; Ábrahám, E.; Hensel, C.; Jansen, N.; Katoen, J.-P.; Quatmann, T.; Volk, M. Parameter synthesis for Markov models: covering the parameter space. Formal Methods in System Design. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10703-023-00442-x\n\n[8] Godlin, B.; Strichman, O. (2009). Regression Verification. DAC. Author manuscript: https://ofers.dds.technion.ac.il/publications/dac09.pdf\n\n[9] Alkassar, E.; Böhme, S.; Mehlhorn, K.; Rizkallah, C. (2013). A Framework for the Verification of Certifying Computations. https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.7462\n\n[10] Necula, G. C.; Schneck, R. R. (2002). Proof-Carrying Code with Untrusted Proof Rules. International Symposium on Software Security. Author manuscript and formalization: https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~necula/ISSS02/\n\n[11] Towards effective metamorphic testing by algorithm stability for linear classification programs. (2021). Journal of Systems and Software, 180, 111012. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2021.111012\n\n[12] Testing and validating machine learning classifiers by metamorphic testing. (2011). Journal of Systems and Software. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0164121210003213\n\n[13] SciPy project. Release news and versions 1.15.3 and 1.18.1. https://scipy.org/news/\n'
        (D / 'V2_REFERENCES.md').write_text(refs, encoding='utf-8')
        report = f"# Phản hồi và kết quả sửa Q1 phiên bản 2\n\n## Các sửa đổi đã thực hiện\n\nP0: Đóng CSV lịch sử trong inputs/historical, ràng buộc SHA-256, bỏ phụ thuộc SUBMISSION_PACKAGE khỏi lệnh chạy mặc định. Kiểm chứng bằng chạy run_all trong thư mục giải nén sạch; xem hồ sơ standalone_reproduction.json để biết kết quả thực thi, không suy ra từ kiểm tra đường dẫn.\n\nP1 semantic independence: Thêm SUT thực thi và 6 source mutants. Checker gọi chương trình thật, giữ cùng hợp đồng tham chiếu và không nhận new_spec. {p['program_runs']} lượt / {p['policy_probes']} probes; {p['nonconforming_runs']} không tuân thủ, snapshot {p['snapshot_detected']}, grid {p['grid_detected']}, guard-complete {p['guard_complete_detected']}. Corpus có điều kiện alpha chỉ đi qua guard được instrument; chưa xác nhận được mọi chương trình tùy ý.\n\nP1 dependency evidence: Đã thực thi hai môi trường SciPy thật 1.15.3/1.18.1 trên {v['fresh_version_comparisons']} bộ đầu vào; {v['policy_divergent_cases']} phân kỳ policy. Không gán chúng thành confirmed bugs. Mục real-SUT/bug corpus bên ngoài vẫn chưa hoàn thành; các đầu vào này kế thừa nghiên cứu trước.\n\nP1 related work: Bổ sung parametric verification/Storm, regression verification, certifying computations/PCC và ML implementation testing, kèm bảng so sánh phạm vi. Đây là khảo sát tập trung mở rộng, chưa phải systematic review và không chứng minh first-in-literature novelty.\n\nP1 manuscript: Mở rộng nội dung về program adapter, dependency versions, proof/trust boundaries, ablation và tái lập; bổ sung 13 nguồn tài liệu được kiểm tra ở mức ghi rõ. Cung cấp DOCX/PDF sau kiểm tra bố cục. Đây là bản thảo nghiên cứu đầy đủ hơn, không chứng nhận sẵn sàng được Q1 chấp nhận.\n\nModerate benchmark: 60 bản ghi ablation trong cùng invocation, chia construction, sweep, witness, assembly/hash và serialization. Các median được tính từ dữ liệu; không trừ hai median độc lập để suy chi phí witness.\n\n## Phân biệt bằng chứng\n\n183/183 của thí nghiệm cũ là declared-semantics verification. 136/136 của thí nghiệm mới là conformance trên chương trình mutant có trace guards đầy đủ. 9/11 version pairs nhạy policy không có nghĩa 9 lỗi SciPy. Không cộng ba denominator này thành một mutation score. Vi phạm ID được báo riêng, và 1/30 kết quả cast float32 của pipeline mới vẫn được giữ.\n"
        (Q / 'RESPONSE_TO_REVIEW_V2.md').write_text(report, encoding='utf-8')
    return _Namespace(**locals())

_sections['build_revision_text'] = _make_build_revision_text()

def _make_build_v3_revision():
    """Integrate external-case, safe-rejection and solver evidence into the paper."""
    import json, csv

    def integrate(Q, text):
        R = Q / 'results/v3'
        e = json.loads((R / 'external_sut_summary.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
        f = json.loads((R / 'failure_and_ci_summary.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
        s = json.loads((R / 'smt_summary.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
        external = f"### Documented external defect and independent upstream code\n\nTo move beyond a locally designed source-mutant corpus, we add scikit-learn issue #23213 [14] and its merged fix #23214 [15]. The confirmed defect concerns truncation of the precision-recall curve after first reaching full recall. A curve consumer can consequently miss lower thresholds that retain all positives while adding further observations. That matters to this paper's explicit secondary objective: among candidates with maximum TP, prefer the largest feasible selected set. The omission is not automatically a defect in every downstream application; we evaluate its propagation under this particular, unchanged consumer contract.\n\nWe freeze the full upstream metrics source file at scikit-learn 1.0.2 commit `7e1e6d09bcc2eaeba98f7e737aac2ac782f0e5f1` and at the PR merge commit `32c53bc69cc0d1f142700dd6c02281a000b855b9`. SHA-256 values, upstream URLs, and BSD license files accompany them. We compile and execute the original `precision_recall_curve` function nodes without rewriting their bodies. The installed scikit-learn 1.9.1 supplies its public counting routine through a compatibility bridge; every returned precision/recall value is checked against direct membership counts. This is isolated historical-source replay, not installation of an old release binary. We also execute the installed current curve implementation and check its returned thresholds against the fixed-source result. A 1.1.0 snapshot inspected during provenance checks still contained the truncation path, so we do not incorrectly label that tag as the fix.\n\nThe frozen inputs comprise the upstream report's synthetic-data/logistic protocol fitted using the current environment, plus the same thirty pipeline workloads used earlier. The report protocol fits and evaluates its classifier on the same rows to expose a metrics defect; no generalization claim follows. One bug exercised on these inputs remains one independently documented defect, not thirty-one independent software projects or defects. Historical fitted coefficients and a historical binary environment were not reproduced.\n\nAll methods receive the same actual upstream threshold outputs and the same exact TP/N feasibility and selected-membership objective. The common consumer adapter compares the complete-group reference family to the returned threshold family. It excludes the plotting sentinel that has no corresponding threshold. It derives exact integer counts from the associated masks rather than mistaking floating-point precision rounding for the upstream truncation bug. This curve-consumer adapter is separate from the local alpha-guard program adapter; it does not imply that arbitrary upstream Python passed our local source-admission policy.\n\n| Evidence | Result |\n|---|---|\n| External projects and upstream-confirmed defects | {e['external_projects']} project, {e['upstream_confirmed_defects']} defect |\n| Input workloads and variant/input executions | {e['input_cases']} workloads, {e['executed_variant_input_pairs']} executions |\n| Affected-source workloads with policy divergence | {e['affected_policy_divergent_inputs']} |\n| Snapshot / grid / full-domain detections on those workloads | {e['snapshot_detected']} / {e['grid_detected']} / {e['complete_detected']} |\n| Fixed-source and installed-current policy false alarms | {e['fixed_or_current_false_alarms']} |\n\nThe original report protocol reproduces a policy difference, with exact PSC 2059/5862 on [0.30,0.90]. Across all thirty-one workloads, fifteen exhibit policy effects; snapshot detects fourteen, while grid and full-domain checking both detect fifteen. Thus this external case establishes useful detection and localization of a documented external defect, but supplies no additional detected case over the thirteen-point grid. We report that null comparison directly. Sixteen workloads show no policy effect in the permitted domain despite the documented library defect. The fixed and current variants conform in all evaluated workloads. We do not pool these workloads with the internal mutant denominators or relabel the nine SciPy version differentials as confirmed bugs.\n\n"
        safety = f"### Fail closed when program assumptions are unsupported\n\nV2 checked alpha placement inside the selection routine, but that syntactic check alone could not exclude state, indirect policy dependencies or unreviewed helpers. The revised adapter uses explicit source admission before execution. Its registry contains hashes of only seven manually reviewed finite programs: the reference and six previously inspected source variants. The adapter additionally rejects direct alpha uses outside the designated guard, global/nonlocal state, top-level mutable state, and unreviewed decorators/defaults. Unknown source is refused even if it appears syntactically harmless. The registry is an auditable compatibility boundary, not an automatic proof that arbitrary Python is safe and not a security sandbox. Hashes do not inspect the semantics of a newly changed executable; an unreviewed source change requires a new review before it can receive a certificate.\n\nRuntime checks require Boolean, correctly aligned membership vectors and valid integer guard counts. Transformed policy arguments, changed guard-event sets and nondeterministic repeated outputs prevent certification. Unsupported input/program behavior is a distinct outcome, not evidence of policy equivalence or a detected regression. The CLI uses exit 0 for a passing checked mode, exit 1 for observed nonconformance, and exit 2 for unsupported operation. Snapshot/grid passes are labeled only as no failure at their probes and are never marked as full-domain certified.\n\nWe test twelve unsupported source fixtures, including hidden state, alpha-dependent candidates/objectives, indirect guard access, dynamic evaluation, random priority, mutable defaults and unreviewed imports. Three additional runtime fixtures test wrong shape, non-Boolean membership and invalid guard arguments. All fifteen are refused without a certificate; all seven reviewed source programs remain admitted. These are designed compatibility/failure tests, not a measured detection rate on adversarial or arbitrary code. A safe refusal may require manual integration work even for a correct third-party program. Source allowlisting limits automation and must be treated as a deployment cost, not hidden behind a claim of general-purpose program verification.\n\n### Local CI execution and frequency context\n\nWe execute actual CLI subprocesses for three pipeline representatives, the reference and strict-precision program, three probe modes and three repetitions, then six complete-mode invocations on a separate controlled N=100,000/G=500 input. The sixty timed processes are accompanied by three negative CLI checks that require exit 2 for unsupported source. All reference jobs pass and every complete-mode strict-precision job fails as expected. Partial-mode outcomes reflect only their evaluated points.\n\nFor the small pipeline representatives, the across-invocation median process time is {f['small_pipeline_median_process_ms']:.2f} ms and median in-gate time is {f['small_pipeline_median_gate_ms']:.2f} ms. On the separate 100,000-row fixture, the corresponding medians are {f['scale_100k_median_process_ms']:.2f} ms and {f['scale_100k_median_gate_ms']:.2f} ms. Process time includes Python startup, imports, input loading and report writing; in-gate time excludes startup and includes repeated-determinism checks, candidate masks and reference comparison. They are paired measurements from these invocations, not directly comparable to the traced compact-certificate benchmark or an estimate for a remote runner. Only three repetitions per program at this scale were measured.\n\nThis is practical local workflow evidence: CI can distinguish pass, regression and unsupported programs and attach a concrete report. It does not establish an acceptable latency budget for every team, the best testing frequency, remote GitHub execution, or a production SLO. A team could choose checks per commit or per release according to its own case count and latency budget; we make no numerical frequency recommendation from one host. Actual deployment across projects remains an external-validity limitation.\n\n"
        smt = f"### Direct SMT baseline on the same equivalence contract\n\nBeyond broad capability comparisons, we encode the external threshold-consumer task directly in Z3 [16]. Alpha is a real variable in [3/10,9/10]. A candidate is feasible under the exact linear inequality alpha*n <= k. Candidates are ordered by their fixed (TP,N) priority, and nested if-then-else terms compute the winning selected count. For this same-score task, candidates from both versions belong to one chain of score-prefix memberships, so different winning counts are equivalent to different selected memberships. That reduction does not apply to arbitrary score reorderings; we do not use it for them.\n\nThe solver asks whether any policy makes the two winning counts unequal. SAT supplies a rational policy witness; UNSAT asserts full-domain equality for the encoded task. Timeout/unknown is never interpreted as equivalence. We independently re-evaluate each SAT model with direct memberships. The complete partition checker separately emits all interval/endpoint evidence and PSC. Both therefore share a Boolean equivalence contract, while their diagnostic outputs differ. The baseline is our explicit finite SMT encoding using an established solver, not a reproduction of a published threshold-testing tool.\n\nAcross {s['distinct_equivalence_tasks']} variant/input tasks and three repetitions, all {s['queries']} solver verdicts agree with partition verification; there are {s['unknown_queries']} unknown outcomes and every SAT policy is independently checked. Persisted SMT-LIB queries are resolved again by the artifact validator. Median encoding time is {s['median_encoding_ms']:.3f} ms and median solving time is {s['median_solver_ms']:.3f} ms across those queries. Counts/masks are prepared before these timers. These measurements do not establish a speed ranking against full interval construction with input validation, hashing, witnesses and JSON serialization. The direct SMT results demonstrate that full-domain Boolean comparison is also attainable with a general reasoning engine, narrowing the defensible contribution to the selector-specific contract, complete diagnostics, independently checkable output and scoped integration.\n\n### Closest method comparison and remaining novelty boundary\n\nThe certifying-algorithm literature explicitly separates an algorithm's mathematical correctness from correctness of a particular computed output [17]. Parameter synthesis in PROPhESY [20] and Storm studies regions for probabilistic properties; parametric monitoring [19] studies parameter-indexed runtime properties; robust classification under uncertain operating conditions [18] studies selection across varying environments. These works establish relevant foundations, not negative capability claims. None should be described as lacking endpoint handling merely because our inspected example targets a different property. A broader systematic search may still uncover a closer selector-specific tool.\n\n| Method considered | Matched question here | Evidence actually compared | Remaining distinction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Snapshot and thirteen-point probes | Membership compatibility at chosen policies | Executed on identical SUT outputs and contract | Cannot certify unprobed policies |\n| Direct Z3 encoding | Existence of any divergent policy on finite nested candidates | 93 actual equivalence tasks, rational counterexample rechecks | One counterexample/Boolean rather than all intervals and PSC |\n| Our partition and checker | Same Boolean task plus full divergence localization | Exact endpoints, intervals and independently replayed memberships | Specialized finite contract and explicit adapter assumptions |\n| Certifying computations [9,17] | Trust in a particular computation and checker | Primary-source conceptual comparison | General foundation, not an empirical competing selector tool |\n| PROPhESY/Storm [6,7,20] | Parameter-region property verification | Primary-source property/input-model comparison | No faithful probabilistic encoding benchmark claimed |\n| Parametric monitoring [19] | Monitoring properties across parameter instances | Primary-source semantic comparison | Object/trace parameters differ from empirical precision breakpoints |\n| Robust classification [18] | Classifier choice as operating conditions vary | Primary-source objective comparison | Does not make the finite-breakpoint idea novel |\n\nWe consequently make no first-in-literature claim for parameter partitioning, regression equivalence or producer/checker separation. The additional same-task SMT experiment is a useful check against overstating novelty: a general solver reaches the same Boolean answers. The proposed research contribution remains the integrated finite selector contract and its endpoint-sensitive diagnostic certificates, scoped executable adapters, and evidence across controlled and one independently documented upstream case. This is stronger substantiation than a scope-only table, but does not certify novelty at a particular journal tier.\n\n"
        start = text.find('| Approach | Domain and boundary treatment |')
        if start >= 0:
            end = text.find('A focused search across these five areas', start)
            if end > start:
                text = text[:start] + text[end:]
        text = text.replace('## 8. RQ4', external + '## 8. RQ4')
        text = text.replace('## 10. Threats', safety + '## 10. Threats')
        text = text.replace('## 12. Conclusion', smt + '## 12. Conclusion')
        text = text.replace('There is no independent institution, prospective deployment or naturally occurring bug corpus.', 'There is no independent institution or prospective deployment. The external study adds one documented upstream defect, not a multi-project naturally occurring bug corpus.')
        text = text.replace('A separate upstream-confirmed bug corpus and deployment study remain work outside the evidence completed here.', 'A broader upstream-confirmed bug corpus and prospective deployment study remain work outside this revision; one isolated-source upstream defect case is now included.')
        text = text.replace('All 19 pytest test items pass', 'All 23 pytest test items pass')
        abstract = '## Abstract\n\nThreshold decision pipelines can preserve a nominal selected output while changing decisions under another allowed precision policy. We characterize exact membership equivalence over a closed policy domain for fixed finite binary observations and a deterministic threshold-selector contract. The method checks critical empirical precision ratios, including endpoint ownership, and exports input-bound interval and observation certificates for an independently implemented checker. Controlled program experiments show differences missed by sampled policies, while a separate external study replays an upstream-confirmed scikit-learn curve-truncation defect through an unchanged policy consumer. On that case, full-domain checking and a thirteen-point grid find the same affected workloads; the method additionally localizes the exact policy region. A direct SMT encoding agrees on every evaluated Boolean equivalence task, showing that full-domain comparison itself is not unique to our partition implementation. The revised executable adapter refuses unreviewed sources and invalid runtime behavior rather than treating unsupported programs as equivalent. Local subprocess measurements assess integration cost, including a 100,000-observation fixture. The evidence supports a specialized, endpoint-correct verification and diagnostic capability. It does not establish arbitrary-program verification, a general real-defect detection rate, independent deployment validation, or first-in-literature novelty of parameter-region reasoning.\n\nKeywords: regression testing; precision constraint; policy equivalence; certifying computation; independently checkable evidence.\n\n'
        text = text[:text.index('## Abstract')] + abstract + text[text.index('## 1. Problem'):]
        refs = '\n[14] scikit-learn issue #23213 (2022). precision_recall_curve is not returning the full curve at high recall. Upstream bug report and maintainer discussion. https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/23213\n\n[15] scikit-learn PR #23214 (2022). FIX compute precision-recall at 100% recall. Merged 2 May 2022. https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/pull/23214\n\n[16] de Moura, L.; Bjørner, N. (2008). Z3: an efficient SMT solver. TACAS. https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/z3-an-efficient-smt-solver/\n\n[17] McConnell, R. M.; Mehlhorn, K.; Näher, S.; Schweitzer, P. (2011). Certifying algorithms. Computer Science Review, 5(2), 119–161. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cosrev.2010.09.009\n\n[18] Provost, F.; Fawcett, T. (2001). Robust Classification for Imprecise Environments. Machine Learning, 42, 203–231. https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0009007\n\n[19] Chen, F.; Roşu, G. (2009). Parametric trace slicing and monitoring. TACAS, 246–261. https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/parametric-trace-slicing-and-monitoring/\n\n[20] Dehnert et al. (2015). PROPhESY: A PRObabilistic ParamEter SYnthesis Tool. CAV. Author manuscript: https://www.cs.ru.nl/personal/nilsjansen/files/publications/dehnert-et-al-cav-2015.pdf\n'
        (Q / 'docs/RELATED_WORK_V3.md').write_text(smt + '\n' + refs, encoding='utf-8')
        (Q / 'docs/EXTERNAL_CASE_AND_FAIL_CLOSED_V3.md').write_text(external + safety, encoding='utf-8')
        return text + '\n' + refs
    return _Namespace(**locals())

_sections['build_v3_revision'] = _make_build_v3_revision()

def _make_build_v4_revision():
    """Integrate measured onboarding, matched diagnostics and checker costs."""
    import json, csv, re
    import numpy as np

    def integrate(Q, text):
        R = Q / 'results/v4'
        on = json.loads((R / 'onboarding_summary.json').read_text())
        cost = list(csv.DictReader((R / 'checker_cost_summary.csv').open()))
        raw = list(csv.DictReader((R / 'complete_region_timings.csv').open()))
        text = text.replace('29 October 2026.**', '30 October 2026.**').replace('All 23 pytest test items pass', 'All 25 pytest test items pass')
        text = re.sub('The comparison below records what is demonstrated.*?directly comparable\\.\\n\\n', '', text, flags=re.S)
        text = text.replace('This is stronger substantiation than a scope-only table, but does not certify novelty at a particular journal tier.', 'This does not certify novelty at a particular journal tier.')
        text = text.replace('| Our partition and checker |', '| SMT predicate-cell enumeration [16,23] | Complete divergent regions, exact PSC and representative witnesses | Eight matched tasks; complete outputs and independent checker agree | Same-score nested candidate encoding and finite feasibility predicates |\n| Our partition and checker |')
        start = text.index('## Abstract')
        end = text.index('Keywords:', start)
        abstract = '## Abstract\n\nRegression tests at a nominal precision constraint can miss changed decisions elsewhere in an allowed policy domain. For a fixed finite binary dataset and deterministic threshold-selector contract, we compute exact selected-membership equivalence, endpoint-sensitive divergent regions, and input-bound certificates. In 210 controlled program runs, full-domain checking detects all 136 nonconforming runs, versus 111 for a thirteen-point grid. A separate replay of one upstream-confirmed scikit-learn defect yields fifteen affected workloads, all detected by both grid and full-domain methods. A matched SMT region-enumeration baseline returns the same complete diagnostics on eight tasks, limiting novelty claims to the specialized contract, diagnostic representation and scoped integration. Public-API onboarding of another selector component conforms at all 59 checked policies; it adds no independent project or confirmed defect. Separate measurements expose substantial certificate-checker cost at 100,000 observations. Immutable release archives are verified through detached hash-bound execution evidence. The results support a specialized verification technique, while multi-project defect validation and broader novelty assessment remain open.\n\n'
        text = text[:start] + abstract + text[end:]
        onboarding = f"### Onboarding a public external selector beyond the source allowlist\n\nWe execute the installed scikit-learn `TunedThresholdClassifierCV` through its documented public API [21]. Its optimizer body is neither extracted nor edited, and it is not admitted by adding another local-program hash. A prefit score-carrier estimator exposes the frozen scores; a custom scorer returns TP*(N+1)+selected_count when the exact rational precision constraint holds and -1 otherwise. The multiplier preserves the lexicographic (TP,N) objective. Complete unique-score thresholds are passed explicitly. The adapter maps an infeasible optimum and the contract's no-positive case to abstention. These are explicit trusted integration choices, not claims about the library's default balanced-accuracy or cross-validation behavior. No predictive generalization or tuning-quality estimate is made from these fixed observations.\n\nThe workload rule is fixed before checking outcomes: seed zero from each of the three existing pipeline families, first 48 rows. WDBC, digits and random forest expose respectively 9, 48 and 13 unique thresholds. All {on['total_policy_probes']} critical-point and midpoint policies conform, with 2,150 actual scorer callbacks and zero membership mismatches. The validator independently rebuilds the exact masks and executes all 59 optimizer fits again. Four configurations are explicitly refused: an integer sampled grid, a missing observed threshold, cross-validation resampling, and refitting. Full-domain coverage follows only under the complete finite candidate and exact scorer contract. It does not extend to arbitrary changes of scorer, interpolation, resampling or estimator state.\n\nObserved onboarding effort consists of {on['adapter_physical_lines']} physical Python lines in the executable study (including its measurement/report code), four public configuration hooks and zero upstream source edits. Source/class hashes, callbacks, decisions and execution times are retained. These are inspectable integration artifacts; programmer hours, independent team effort and maintenance costs were not measured. The scorer, score carrier and abstention mapping remain trusted glue.\n\nWe also inspect a second independently authored project, eval-toolkit [22], at commit `a6400e74c4534d6d79f17dee11ad903b6d664980`. Its source, tests, license and packaging metadata are hash-pinned. Its Python >=3.13 requirement is incompatible with our Python 3.12.10 environment. We record unsupported runtime, do not execute its package, and issue no policy certificate. This is a documented onboarding barrier, not a negative correctness result. The completed additional selector case is therefore a new component in the same external scikit-learn project; the executed external-project count remains one and the confirmed-defect count remains one.\n\n"
        regionrows = []
        for variant in ['affected', 'fixed']:
            for method in ['partition', 'smt_all_regions']:
                rr = [r for r in raw if r['variant'] == variant and r['method'] == method]
                producer = [float(r['producer_and_serialization_ms']) for r in rr]
                checker = [float(r['independent_checker_ms']) for r in rr]
                regionrows.append(f'| {variant} | {method} | {np.median(producer):.3f} | {np.median(checker):.3f} |')
        regions = "### Matched complete-region diagnostics, including certification costs\n\nThe Boolean SMT comparison above is extended to the same diagnostic contract as a finite partition adapter: all maximal connected divergent components with exact endpoint ownership, exact PSC, a separate Boolean equivalence flag, and one representative policy/row witness per component. A representative witness is existential at its reported policy; it is not one observation that changes throughout the whole component. A point-only difference retains non-equivalence even when PSC is zero. Two added tests exercise that case and the empty-divergence case.\n\nThe second algorithm is an explicit Z3 QF_LRA encoding with finite predicate-cell enumeration. Each SAT model identifies a cell of constant feasibility truth; the algorithm blocks that entire cell and repeats until residual UNSAT. Blocking individual real-valued model points would not establish termination. There are finitely many empirical-ratio cuts, so at most their number plus two solver checks are allowed. This uses established AllSMT-style predicate reasoning [23]; MathSAT's reference documents AllSat over important atoms, but MathSAT itself was not benchmarked. Timeout or unknown prevents certification.\n\nBoth implementations receive identical prepared nested threshold tables. They use the same witness representation, serialization and independent membership-based coverage checker. The issue protocol and seed zero of each existing pipeline family provide four fixed representatives; affected and fixed variants yield eight tasks. Three repetitions per algorithm produce 48 measurements. Every complete output agrees; sixteen first-repetition certificates persist and are replayed independently. Rows below are descriptive medians across four workloads times three repetitions per variant/method, not independent defect counts.\n\n| Variant | Method | Region construction + JSON (ms) | Independent checking (ms) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n" + '\n'.join(regionrows) + '\n\nPer-task raw records retain the differing input sizes, region counts, solver calls, certificate bytes and checker probes. Counts/mask preparation is excluded for both methods; representative witness construction and serialization are included in producer time. Input loading and whole-release validation are excluded. These timings describe the generic external-table partition adapter, not the faster rank-based comparison core. The shared checker can dominate either producer. No universal speed ranking, unique ability to find complete regions, or new general parameter-synthesis principle follows. The remaining contribution is the explicit precision-selector contract and diagnostic format, its checked implementation and scoped software integration.\n\n'
        cr = []
        for r in cost:
            cr.append(f"| {int(r['n']):,} / {r['g']} | {r['workload']} | {float(r['checker_median_ms']):.2f} ({float(r['checker_iqr_ms']):.2f}) | {float(r['parse_median_ms']):.3f} | {float(r['separate_peak_aux_mib']):.2f} |")
        check = '### Independent certificate consumption at scale\n\nThe compact producer timings do not measure the independent verifier. We therefore time certificate consumption separately on invariant and divergent controlled fixtures at N/G=1,000/20, 10,000/100 and 100,000/500. The certificate and input arrays are prepared before measurement. After a warmup, five untraced repetitions time JSON parsing separately from full independent verification, including candidate reconstruction, hashes, coverage, PSC and witness checks. A separate sixth run measures traced peak auxiliary memory. Its time is not pooled with the untraced repetitions. The 36 raw records comprise thirty timings and six memory measurements; medians and linear IQRs are recomputed by the artifact validator.\n\n| N / G | Fixture | Checker median (IQR), ms | JSON median, ms | Separate peak auxiliary MiB |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n' + '\n'.join(cr) + '\n\nThese costs are materially larger than compact production costs, especially for the divergent 100,000-row fixture. The independent implementation first materializes Boolean threshold masks before bitset packing; its peak scratch therefore includes O(NG) bytes, while packed memberships use O(NG) bits. Python integer operations have operand-size costs. The verifier is deliberately independent of the production heap/rank comparator, but it is not a constant-cost or universally cheap CI consumer. Five repetitions on one host and one separate memory run per group do not support a deployment SLO. A team must budget producer, checker, process startup and its actual case count separately.\n\n'
        release = "### Immutable final-archive reproduction\n\nEarlier evidence described a candidate archive and matching source hashes. That is insufficient to claim execution of a differently hashed final ZIP. We close the historical V3 gap by extracting the exact delivered archive, SHA-256 `e45f431d77bd8102376aafd43c558b01a398cdbf98d9bb919ef079d90a7671c8`, and executing all fifteen stages successfully without an adjacent original submission. The detached report records the environment, each exit status, stage logs, output digests and unchanged archive hash. It verifies 1,219 manifest entries and hashes 1,001 output files. This demonstrates a fresh run of the actual V3 release, not independent human replication or identical wall-clock measurements.\n\nThe V4 release follows the same non-circular protocol with nineteen stages. Freeze source, measured tables and rendered reading copies first; package once; execute the extracted final ZIP; then publish its execution evidence outside the archive. `verify_release.py` accepts an archive, new work root, detached report directory and expected stage count. Its report binds the archive hash before and after execution. The new execution's raw timings and rebuilt Markdown are evidence in the extraction, while the frozen reading copy retains its original measured run. Timing values may differ. A report is not inserted into the archive whose hash it records, and the archive is not repackaged after verification. The actual V4 pass/fail outcome and digest belong to the detached release report delivered with the archive, not a self-referential claim in this manuscript.\n\n"
        text = text.replace('## 10. Threats', onboarding + check + '## 10. Threats')
        text = text.replace('## 12. Conclusion', regions + release + '## 12. Conclusion')
        text = text.replace('O(N*G) bits for stored membership masks', 'O(N*G) bits for packed membership masks, with O(N*G) bytes of Boolean scratch in this implementation')
        refs = '\n[21] scikit-learn developers. TunedThresholdClassifierCV API: callable scorer, explicit threshold arrays and prefit configuration. https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.model_selection.TunedThresholdClassifierCV.html (accessed 30 October 2026).\n\n[22] Behring, B. eval-toolkit: threshold selection methodology and source. https://brandon-behring.github.io/eval-toolkit/methodology/thresholds.html ; pinned packaging/source at https://github.com/brandon-behring/eval-toolkit/tree/a6400e74c4534d6d79f17dee11ad903b6d664980 (accessed 30 October 2026).\n\n[23] MathSAT developers. API reference: msat_all_sat, enumeration over important atoms. https://mathsat.fbk.eu/apireference.html (accessed 30 October 2026). Used to contextualize predicate enumeration, not as an evaluated implementation.\n'
        (Q / 'docs/V4_ADDITIONAL_EVIDENCE.md').write_text(onboarding + regions + check + release + refs, encoding='utf-8')
        return text + '\n' + refs
    return _Namespace(**locals())

_sections['build_v4_revision'] = _make_build_v4_revision()

def _make_build_v5_revision():
    """Integrate independently replayable V5 evidence without inflating bug counts."""
    import json, csv, re, xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
    import numpy as np

    def vietnamese_report(Q):
        return '# Kết quả Q1 V5 ngày 30/10/2026\n\nĐã thực hiện các hướng theo phản biện V4. Bản đọc hiện tại là docs/MANUSCRIPT_Q1_V5.pdf và .docx; nguồn nội dung là docs/MANUSCRIPT_Q1.md.\n\n- Checker streaming được viết độc lập với production/legacy checker, kiểm tra đủ 428 certificates và 41.348 probes. Phép đo ghép cặp 72 bản ghi (60 timing, 12 memory), tới 100.000 mẫu, giữ cùng nghĩa vụ hash/coverage/witness. Xem bảng thực đo results/v5/paired_checker_summary.csv.\n- Dự án thứ hai eval-toolkit 1.12.0 được cài từ wheel nguồn nguyên vẹn tại commit ghim, chạy bằng Python 3.13.18. 18 upstream tests PASS. Trên 30 workload, 884 policy probes phát hiện 27 khác biệt ngữ nghĩa với oracle hữu tỉ; 196 probes tại policy biểu diễn được có khác biệt ở 19 workload. Không gọi các khác biệt này là confirmed bugs.\n- Đối chứng complete-region mở rộng 27 task factorial, 270 timing records, 54 certificates, 0–39 components; mọi đầu ra partition/SMT khớp. Tám task external trước được giữ riêng.\n- Bổ sung lập luận soundness của streaming và biên làm tròn, Related Work gần hơn và RQ5–RQ7.\n- Báo cáo chạy đúng ZIP V4 đủ 19 stages nằm trong evidence/V4_FINAL_RELEASE_EVIDENCE. V5 dùng 23 stages, bằng chứng chạy trên đúng ZIP cuối được giao tách rời cùng gói delivery.\n\nCác số đo lịch sử Python 3.12 và thí nghiệm V5 Python 3.13 được phân biệt. Không lấy tỷ số producer/checker từ hai giao thức khác nhau. Có hai dự án ngoài thực thi nhưng vẫn chỉ một upstream-confirmed defect; eval-toolkit dùng scikit-learn làm phụ thuộc. Chưa chứng minh independent deployment, multi-project defect-detection rate hoặc sẵn sàng nộp Q1. Xem RESPONSE_TO_REVIEW_V5.md và docs/REPRODUCIBILITY_V5.md.\n'

    def integrate(Q, text):
        R = Q / 'results/v5'
        read = lambda p: json.loads(p.read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
        second = read(R / 'second_project_summary.json')
        paired = list(csv.DictReader((R / 'paired_checker_summary.csv').open()))
        raw = list(csv.DictReader((R / 'expanded_region_raw.csv').open()))
        expanded = read(R / 'expanded_region_summary.json')
        tests = sum((int(t.get('tests', 0)) for t in ET.parse(Q / 'results/pytest.xml').iter('testsuite')))
        text = text.replace('All 25 pytest test items pass', f'All {tests} core pytest test items pass')
        text = text.replace('Research manuscript revised after technical review on 30 October 2026.', 'Research manuscript V5 revised after technical review on 30 October 2026.')
        text = text.replace('Eight matched tasks; complete outputs and independent checker agree', 'Eight external and 27 controlled tasks; complete outputs and checker agree')
        text = text.replace('It adds universal policy-domain comparison, endpoint-sensitive certificates, a domain-length metric, and controlled selector/numerical mutations.', 'It adds universal policy-domain comparison, endpoint-sensitive certificates, a domain-length metric, controlled selector/numerical mutations, and a separately implemented streaming consumer. The revision also makes binary64 policy boundaries explicit and checks integration on two externally authored projects.')
        text = text.replace('## 3. Contract', 'RQ5: How do partition and SMT compare when both must produce identical complete regions, endpoint ownership, PSC and representative witnesses, across input size, candidate count and divergent-component structure?\n\nRQ6: Can a separately implemented streaming consumer preserve all certificate obligations while reducing paired verification time and auxiliary memory?\n\nRQ7: What diagnostic differences arise when a second external project uses a binary64 public policy interface instead of an exact rational contract, and which of these are software defects?\n\n## 3. Contract', 1)
        a = text.index('## Abstract')
        b = text.index('Keywords:', a)
        abstract = '## Abstract\n\nSampled precision policies can miss changed threshold decisions elsewhere in an allowed domain. We compute exact finite-input membership equivalence, endpoint-sensitive divergent regions and independently checkable certificates under an explicit selector contract. Controlled program experiments detect 136 nonconforming runs, versus 111 with a thirteen-point grid. One documented scikit-learn defect affects fifteen workloads, all found by both methods. A second installed external project, eval-toolkit, exposes binary64 policy boundaries: 27 of thirty fixed workloads differ from a rational reference in narrow regions missed by the grid; these are semantic differences, not confirmed upstream defects. Partition and SMT return identical complete diagnostics on eight external tasks and 27 controlled scaling tasks. A separate streaming certificate consumer preserves the checked obligations while avoiding retention of all threshold masks; paired measurements quantify its time and memory tradeoff through 100,000 observations. Immutable release archives are executed before detached hash-bound evidence is delivered. The contribution is specialized contract-aware diagnosis and scoped integration; general parameter-region reasoning, deployment readiness and multi-project defect-detection rates are not established.\n\n'
        text = text[:a] + abstract + text[b:]
        old = 'We also inspect a second independently authored project, eval-toolkit'
        start = text.find(old)
        if start >= 0:
            end = text.find('\n\n', start)
            text = text[:start] + "The preceding public-API study concerns a second component in scikit-learn. V4 additionally inspected eval-toolkit but did not execute it because its Python >=3.13 requirement conflicted with V4's Python 3.12.10 environment. That historical refusal is retained in the V4 results. V5 uses a separate Python 3.13.18 environment and executes the installed second project as described below; the former compatibility barrier is not represented as an executed case." + text[end:]
        cr = []
        for n in [1000, 10000, 100000]:
            for work in ['invariant', 'divergent']:
                aa = next((r for r in paired if int(r['n']) == n and r['workload'] == work and (r['method'] == 'legacy')))
                bb = next((r for r in paired if int(r['n']) == n and r['workload'] == work and (r['method'] == 'streamed')))
                cr.append(f"| {n:,} / {work} | {float(aa['median_ms']):.2f} ({float(aa['iqr_ms']):.2f}) | {float(bb['median_ms']):.2f} ({float(bb['iqr_ms']):.2f}) | {float(aa['peak_aux_mib']):.2f} | {float(bb['peak_aux_mib']):.2f} |")
        checker = "### RQ6 Streaming certificate consumption\n\nThe legacy verifier first materializes all Boolean threshold masks. Its independently measured cost motivated a second consumer, not a change to the producer or finite partition theorem. The new implementation imports neither production code nor the legacy checker. It counts one threshold mask at a time, retaining only exact ratios, actual TP/N and candidate descriptors. A reverse suffix-maximum table over ascending feasibility ratios supplies exhaustive-priority winners by exact bisection. It reconstructs and packs only the current winning mask for each version. It uses no production priority heap or rank-containment comparator and retains no all-candidate bitset cache.\n\nThe suffix winner argument is direct: feasible candidates form a suffix (lower bound for inclusive feasibility, upper bound for strict feasibility), and reverse induction computes that suffix's maximum fixed priority. Independent critical-point construction, ordered certificate coverage and endpoint ownership then justify checking all atom representatives. A forward cell cursor validates each probe; actual bitsets validate equality, witness IDs/memberships and changed-row counts. Exact divergent length and PSC are independently recomputed. Detailed lemmas and conditional soundness are in STREAMING_CHECKER_AND_FLOAT_CUT_PROOFS.md. These are mathematical arguments plus tests, not a machine-checked proof or independent human replication.\n\nThe consumer uses O(N+G+K+C) auxiliary descriptors/probes and current-mask storage, excluding arbitrary-precision operand sizes, for K critical values and C certificate cells. Direct counting and winner reconstruction retain worst-case O(NG) work; split-tie ordering adds O(N log N), with ratio/query/probe sorting costs stated in the proof supplement. In particular, G=N can still be quadratic. Suffix maxima, bisection and streaming are established techniques [17], not new general primitives.\n\nAll 428 frozen application/mutation certificates are accepted under the same obligations as the legacy checker, covering 41,348 probes. New exhaustive small-input comparisons exercise seven specifications; tamper tests reject altered bindings, partitions, winners and metrics, and retain point-only non-equivalence. Thirty core tests pass; the upstream project's eighteen tests are counted separately.\n\nThe paired experiment regenerates the same six N/G fixtures as the earlier checker study, prepares an identical certificate/payload outside timing, warms up both consumers, and randomizes their order within each of five repetitions. JSON parsing is recorded separately. A sixth invocation per consumer/group traces memory independently. The sixty untraced timing records and twelve separate memory records are never pooled. Each row below compares identical inputs, policies, coverage, hash and witness obligations; these are not ratios against the differently instrumented producer benchmark.\n\n| N / fixture | Legacy median (IQR), ms | Streaming median (IQR), ms | Legacy peak MiB | Streaming peak MiB |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n" + '\n'.join(cr) + '\n\nMemory peaks are auxiliary allocations with inputs/certificate prepared in advance and one traced run per group. Five timings on one host do not establish a universal CI budget. The implementation preserves a deliberately different verification route while reducing retained masks; shared dependencies and mathematical assumptions remain trusted. Results show an implementation tradeoff under this contract, not a general theorem that checking is always cheaper than production.\n\n'
        text = re.sub('### Independent certificate consumption at scale\\n.*?(?=## 10\\. Threats)', checker, text, flags=re.S)
        text = text.replace('Thirty core tests pass', f'{tests} core tests pass')
        scaling = []
        wins = 0
        for case in sorted({r['case_id'] for r in raw}):
            aa = [r for r in raw if r['case_id'] == case and r['method'] == 'partition']
            bb = [r for r in raw if r['case_id'] == case and r['method'] == 'smt_all_regions']
            pa = np.median([float(r['total_checked_ms']) for r in aa])
            pb = np.median([float(r['total_checked_ms']) for r in bb])
            wins += pa < pb
            if aa[0]['n'] == '100000':
                scaling.append(f"| {aa[0]['g']} / {aa[0]['pattern']} | {aa[0]['critical_points']} | {aa[0]['divergent_components']} | {pa:.2f} | {pb:.2f} |")
        regions = f"### RQ5 Diagnostic-complete comparison across workload structure\n\nBoth implementations must return the same maximal divergent components, exact endpoint ownership, exact PSC, separate Boolean equivalence and one representative policy/row witness per component. A representative witness is existential at its reported policy, not one observation changing throughout a component. SMT repeatedly finds a QF_LRA model, expands it to its complete feasibility-predicate cell, blocks that cell, and finishes only at residual UNSAT. Unknown/timeout prevents certification. General predicate enumeration is established [23-25]; complete diagnostics are not a unique capability of our partition method.\n\nThe eight V4 external affected/fixed tasks remain a separate check and still agree. We add a prespecified factorial design: N in {{1,000,10,000,100,000}}, G in {{10,30,90}}, and no, one-middle, or alternating candidate omission. Scores form G tied groups with gradually decreasing group-positive proportions; fixed-seed permutations remove dependence on row order. These designed families yield {expanded['component_range'][0]}–{expanded['component_range'][1]} divergent components. They are controlled tasks, not new upstream defects or independent deployments. Every one of the 27 tasks is retained, including empty-divergence controls.\n\nFive repetitions per method/task, with randomized method order inside each repetition, produce 270 timing records and 54 saved certificates. Both methods receive the same candidate family, regenerate counts/masks during each timed preparation, emit the same diagnostic JSON and invoke the same independent region checker. All complete outputs agree and every saved certificate is replayed. Preparation, producer plus serialization, checker, total checked cost, candidate counts, exact critical-point count, divergent components, probes, bytes and solver calls are retained separately. Thus input-size effects are not inferred from a preparation-excluded timer alone.\n\nThe table shows N=100,000; the raw factorial records preserve the other scales. Total includes fresh preparation, complete diagnostic production/JSON and independent checking. Entries are within-task medians of five runs, not pooled medians across heterogeneous tasks.\n\n| G / omission | Critical points | Components | Partition total ms | SMT total ms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n" + '\n'.join(scaling) + f'\n\nAcross all 27 designed tasks, partition has the smaller total median on {wins}; the other {27 - wins} have an equal or smaller SMT median. These descriptive comparisons do not establish statistical significance, superiority for arbitrary selectors or parity with an optimized general AllSMT solver. This is our direct Z3 encoding and generic external-table partition adapter, not a reproduction of TabularAllSMT or a benchmark of the compact rank-based producer. The region checker still enumerates prepared memberships and is distinct from the new streaming consumer for the core certificate format. Evidence supports scoped cost characterization and common diagnostic correctness; broader performance claims remain unsupported.\n\n'
        text = re.sub('### Matched complete-region diagnostics, including certification costs\\n.*?(?=### Immutable final-archive reproduction)', '', text, flags=re.S)
        text = text.replace('## 9. Integration', regions + '## 9. Integration', 1)
        external = f"### RQ7 An executed second project and its actual policy semantics\n\nWe build a wheel from the complete, unmodified eval-toolkit 1.12.0 source at commit `a6400e74c4534d6d79f17dee11ad903b6d664980`, retaining hashes, its MIT license and package metadata. Python 3.13.18 satisfies its >=3.13 requirement. The installed TargetPrecisionSelector is called through its actual public API; no method body is extracted, patched or replaced with a custom scorer. All eighteen packaged upstream threshold tests pass. The package and source are distributed for offline installation. The two externally authored projects executed across the studies are now scikit-learn and eval-toolkit, although the latter depends on scikit-learn's precision-recall routine; this is not independent numerical infrastructure or independent institutional deployment.\n\nThe workload rule is fixed: first 64 rows of all thirty existing pipeline inputs, with no positive-case filtering. The wrapper passes float(alpha) to the public precision parameter and maps only the documented no-feasible RuntimeError to abstention. Its exact-reference objective is still maximum (TP,N); in the same nested family, this equals the project's smallest-feasible-threshold objective. Input validation, source identity and candidate counts are checked. Actual floating precision values are compared against direct integer membership counts.\n\nIt would be unsound to assume a binary64 guard changes exactly at the rational ratio k/n. For an actual binary64 precision p, let q=nextafter(p,+infinity) and c=(exact(p)+exact(q))/2. The public predicate p >= float(alpha) changes at c, including equality exactly when p has the even significand. The proof follows monotone nearest/even rounding and is a selector-specific application of established rounding-preimage reasoning [26]. We explicitly check midpoint ownership and both neighboring cells on the runtime. The complete critical set is the union of rational-reference cuts, these rounding-cell cuts and domain endpoints. A model of these guards is checked against every actual public-API output, including all endpoints; unsupported numerical behavior must prevent certification.\n\nAcross thirty workloads, {second['policy_probes']} critical-point/midpoint policies and {second['public_calls']} public invocations (including the grid/snapshot baselines) are executed. The guard model matches every actual output. Twenty-seven workloads have narrow differences from the exact rational reference; neither snapshot nor the thirteen-point grid detects any of them. These are twenty-seven semantic differentials, not twenty-seven bugs or regressions between upstream releases. The library documents a floating policy interface, so the rational reference is a caller-selected stronger/different contract, not an upstream requirement it is shown to violate. The upstream-confirmed defect count remains one; on that documented scikit-learn defect, incremental detection over grid remains zero.\n\nEach input-bound certificate preserves exact rounding-cut fractions, ownership, actual/reference bitsets, row witnesses, complete divergent regions and PSC. The validator calls the installed selector again at all 884 policies and checks every stored field. We additionally probe actual representable binary64 precision policies: {second['representable_policy_probes']} such probes expose differences on {second['representable_policy_divergent_workloads']} workloads. Grid/snapshot targets are exact rational fractions, whereas this additional group embeds each binary64 value by its exact integer ratio; it does not reinterpret a decimal grid target as its binary approximation. Thus the continuous-domain finding is not automatically equated to a difference at a policy the float API can represent. With labels [1,1,0,0,0] ordered by decreasing distinct score, the representable float 0.4 exceeds exact 2/5: at the exact rational embedding of that float, the float selector admits all five while the rational reference admits four. At exact decimal 2/5 both admit five. This is a designed explanation of distinct policy contracts, not a newly documented upstream bug. Tiny interval length is not a deployment probability or impact estimate. Adoption should decide whether binary64 semantics are acceptable, rather than treating every differential as a release-blocking defect.\n\n"
        text = text.replace('## 10. Threats', external + '## 10. Threats')
        text = text.replace('The V4 release follows the same non-circular protocol with nineteen stages.', 'The V4 release also completed nineteen stages on its exact ZIP, SHA-256 `f45403ce8011adc3d084af2b432953286072fd8b6302e134ab1e087d4075b835`, with unchanged before/after hashes, 1,269 manifest files and 1,037 result-output digests. Its detached report and stage logs are included under evidence/V4_FINAL_RELEASE_EVIDENCE in this revision; they must accompany the old archive rather than be inferred from its historical internal status file. The V5 release follows the same non-circular protocol with twenty-three stages under Python 3.13.18.')
        text = text.replace('The actual V4 pass/fail outcome and digest belong to the detached release report delivered with the archive', 'The actual V5 pass/fail outcome and digest belong to the detached release report delivered with the archive')
        text = text.replace('Freeze source, measured tables and rendered reading copies first;', 'The new archive omits stale root run_all status/log files; fresh execution creates its own. Freeze source, measured tables and rendered reading copies first;')
        closest = "### Closest algorithmic overlap and remaining contribution\n\nThe closest overlap is concrete. Morettin et al. [24] use SMT predicate abstraction to enumerate models for weighted integration; our uniform one-dimensional PSC is not a new general integration principle. Spallitta et al. [25] develop disjoint projected enumeration with more sophisticated solver techniques than our blocking-cell baseline. We therefore do not interpret a win over our encoding as a win over state-of-the-art AllSMT. Andrlon's rounding-preimage analysis [26] establishes a wider arithmetic foundation than the single guard mapping used here. Certifying-computation frameworks [9,17] already motivate a simple independent consumer. Suffix maxima and one-mask-at-a-time processing are implementation choices within that tradition. These sources narrow the proposed contribution to the fully specified precision-selector contract, exact boundary-aware diagnostic artifacts, an independently implemented consumer with measured tradeoffs, and checked integration on two external projects. They do not establish first-in-literature novelty of partitioning, rounding preimages, enumeration or streaming.\n\n"
        text = text.replace('## 12. Conclusion', closest + '## 12. Conclusion')
        conclusion = "## 12. Conclusion\n\nThe specified finite selector contract supports exact membership comparison with explicit ownership of policy endpoints and independently replayable diagnostic evidence. Partition and SMT both produce complete regions; the expanded matched study characterizes their costs rather than assigning exclusive capability to one method. A separately implemented streaming consumer preserves the certificate obligations and reduces measured retained-memory and checking costs on paired fixtures, while retaining worst-case O(NG) scanning work. Executing two external projects demonstrates an upstream defect's policy propagation and the separate effect of binary64 interface semantics. The latter differences require an explicit caller contract before they can be treated as regressions. One confirmed defect, shared numerical dependencies and one-host timings leave broader bug-detection rates, deployment budgets and first-in-literature novelty unresolved.\n\n"
        text = re.sub('## 12\\. Conclusion\\n.*?(?=## Reproducibility and declarations)', conclusion, text, flags=re.S)
        refs = '\n[24] Morettin, P.; Passerini, A.; Sebastiani, R. (2017). Efficient Weighted Model Integration via SMT-Based Predicate Abstraction. IJCAI. Author manuscript: https://sml.disi.unitn.it/papers/ijcai17.pdf\n\n[25] Spallitta, G.; Sebastiani, R.; Biere, A. (2025). Disjoint projected enumeration for SAT and SMT without blocking clauses. Artificial Intelligence, 345, 104346. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.artint.2025.104346\n\n[26] Andrlon, M. (2023). Finding Normal Binary Floating-Point Factors Efficiently. Journal of Automated Reasoning, 67, article 11. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10817-023-09659-1\n'
        (Q / 'docs/V5_ADDITIONAL_EVIDENCE.md').write_text(checker + regions + external + closest + refs, encoding='utf-8')
        return text + '\n' + refs
    return _Namespace(**locals())

_sections['build_v5_revision'] = _make_build_v5_revision()

def _make_build_v6_revision():
    """Targeted revision: operational scope, additional defect and grouped RQs."""
    import json, csv, re
